 "captions": [
  {
   "id": "B1.c1",
   "beat": "B1",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 4.6,
   "e": 10.6,
   "text": "Every 30 seconds of a night's sleep gets a label: Wake, N1, N2, N3 or REM. Experts score them by hand."
  },
  {
   "id": "B1.c2",
   "beat": "B1",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 11.3,
   "e": 16.1,
   "text": "Can a computer read sleep from one EEG channel, a single pair of scalp electrodes?"
  },
  {
   "id": "P1.c1",
   "beat": "P1",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 17.2,
   "e": 22,
   "text": "One pipeline. Input: a single Fpz-Cz EEG channel, 100 samples a second, all night."
  },
  {
   "id": "P1.c2",
   "beat": "P1",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 22.7,
   "e": 28.2,
   "text": "Process: cut into 30-second epochs, make a frequency picture of each, then a CNN and a BiGRU."
  },
  {
   "id": "P1.c3",
   "beat": "P1",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 28.9,
   "e": 32.5,
   "text": "Output: one of five stages for every 30 seconds, a hypnogram."
  },
  {
   "id": "B2.c1",
   "beat": "B2",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 34.4,
   "e": 40,
   "text": "A 2026 Scientific Reports paper claims about 83% accuracy from one channel, chaining four ideas."
  },
  {
   "id": "B2.c2",
   "beat": "B2",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 40.7,
   "e": 44.8,
   "text": "We set out to reproduce it. Within our time and compute, we could not."
  },
  {
   "id": "B2.c3",
   "beat": "B2",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 45.5,
   "e": 50.8,
   "text": "The paper splits epochs at random, so one person can be on both sides. We split by person."
  },
  {
   "id": "B2.c4",
   "beat": "B2",
   "c": 4,
   "s": 51.5,
   "e": 56.5,
   "text": "Why? A new patient is a new person; a random split can reward recognising the person."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3.c1",
   "beat": "B3",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 57.6,
   "e": 64.4,
   "text": "So we changed the question: does it still work on a person the model has never seen, and can we explain every step?"
  },
  {
   "id": "Q2.c1",
   "beat": "Q2",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 65.9,
   "e": 72,
   "text": "Scope: not a replication or clinical validation. Twenty people, one public dataset, unseen people only."
  },
  {
   "id": "Q2.c2",
   "beat": "Q2",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 72.7,
   "e": 79.3,
   "text": "Hypothesis, formed after the baseline failed: nights differ in signal strength, so per-night scaling should help."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3b.c1",
   "beat": "B3b",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 80.7,
   "e": 87.2,
   "text": "Sleep-EDF, public on PhysioNet: healthy adults recorded at home on cassette recorders, usually two nights each."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3b.c2",
   "beat": "B3b",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 87.9,
   "e": 94.7,
   "text": "Trained technicians scored every 30-second page of each night by hand, using the 1968 Rechtschaffen and Kales rules."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3b.c3",
   "beat": "B3b",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 95.4,
   "e": 100,
   "text": "Two experts can disagree, so labels are a trusted reference, not perfect truth."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3b.c4",
   "beat": "B3b",
   "c": 4,
   "s": 100.7,
   "e": 106.3,
   "text": "We use 20 people, 39 nights, 42,307 epochs. Trimmed, a night is about 1,085 epochs, or 9 hours."
  },
  {
   "id": "D2.c1",
   "beat": "D2",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 107.4,
   "e": 114.3,
   "text": "Three variables: the EEG signal, the stage label, and a person ID that only splits the data and never feeds the model."
  },
  {
   "id": "D2.c2",
   "beat": "D2",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 115,
   "e": 121.9,
   "text": "Limits: healthy adults only, just 20 people, human-scored labels and one channel. This data cannot speak for patients."
  },
  {
   "id": "M1.c1",
   "beat": "M1",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 123,
   "e": 128.8,
   "text": "STFT: a 2-second window slides in 1-second steps, making a 129 × 29 picture of frequency over time."
  },
  {
   "id": "M1.c2",
   "beat": "M1",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 129.5,
   "e": 135,
   "text": "Why? Stages are defined by rhythms: slow waves mark N3, spindles N2, alpha waves relaxed wake."
  },
  {
   "id": "M1.c3",
   "beat": "M1",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 135.7,
   "e": 143.5,
   "text": "The paper uses a synchrosqueezed transform. We chose STFT because we can draw, compute and check it by hand. We never compared them."
  },
  {
   "id": "M2.c1",
   "beat": "M2",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 144.3,
   "e": 151.5,
   "text": "CNN: three blocks of 32, 64 and 128 channels squeeze each picture into 128 numbers. Its limit: it sees one epoch at a time."
  },
  {
   "id": "M3.c1",
   "beat": "M3",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 152.6,
   "e": 158.4,
   "text": "BiGRU: two layers read 20 neighbouring epochs forwards and backwards, because sleep is a sequence."
  },
  {
   "id": "M3.c2",
   "beat": "M3",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 159.1,
   "e": 164.4,
   "text": "Why? Sleep comes in long runs: an epoch is easier to judge with its neighbours than alone."
  },
  {
   "id": "M3.c3",
   "beat": "M3",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 165.1,
   "e": 169.7,
   "text": "The cost: it uses future epochs, so it works offline only, never in real time."
  },
  {
   "id": "M4.c1",
   "beat": "M4",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 170.4,
   "e": 176.3,
   "text": "Contrastive learning trains the CNN: similar epochs are pulled together, different ones pushed apart."
  },
  {
   "id": "M4.c2",
   "beat": "M4",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 177,
   "e": 181.8,
   "text": "It is a second training phase with many settings to tune: more time, more compute."
  },
  {
   "id": "M4.c3",
   "beat": "M4",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 182.5,
   "e": 189.9,
   "text": "We dropped the contrastive stage: reproducing it failed, and we wanted a chain we can check step by step. We never tested it."
  },
  {
   "id": "M5.c1",
   "beat": "M5",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 190.8,
   "e": 197.2,
   "text": "Testing is by person: ten folds, 16 people train, 2 pick the checkpoint, 2 are tested. Nobody appears twice."
  },
  {
   "id": "M5.c2",
   "beat": "M5",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 197.9,
   "e": 201.5,
   "text": "Only two test people per fold, so scores swing between folds."
  },
  {
   "id": "E1.c1",
   "beat": "E1",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 202.3,
   "e": 208.9,
   "text": "Baseline first: macro-F1 56.42%, 95% interval 48.26 to 63.44. Accuracy 65.00%. Folds swing from 39.95% to 74.39%."
  },
  {
   "id": "E1.c2",
   "beat": "E1",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 209.6,
   "e": 215.8,
   "text": "Opening single nights, three were right on only 7.22%, 18.60% and 19.01% of epochs. The average hid them."
  },
  {
   "id": "E2.c1",
   "beat": "E2",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 216.5,
   "e": 222.2,
   "text": "To fix this, R1 changes one step: how each frequency picture is scaled before it reaches the CNN."
  },
  {
   "id": "E2.c2",
   "beat": "E2",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 222.9,
   "e": 229.5,
   "text": "STFT hands the CNN one picture per epoch. We first rescale each frequency row, so every frequency counts evenly."
  },
  {
   "id": "E2.c3",
   "beat": "E2",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 230.2,
   "e": 236.3,
   "text": "That rescaling is a z-score: subtract the mean, divide by the spread. Values become standard deviations."
  },
  {
   "id": "E2.c4",
   "beat": "E2",
   "c": 4,
   "s": 237,
   "e": 243.3,
   "text": "The baseline already did this, but with one ruler for all nights: the 16 training people's mean and spread."
  },
  {
   "id": "E2.c5",
   "beat": "E2",
   "c": 5,
   "s": 244,
   "e": 249.3,
   "text": "R1 gives every night its own ruler: that night's own mean and spread, with no labels used."
  },
  {
   "id": "E2.c6",
   "beat": "E2",
   "c": 6,
   "s": 250,
   "e": 256.9,
   "text": "Same architecture, retrained: macro-F1 74.86%, interval 71.33 to 78.03; accuracy 80.99%. Gain +18.44 (10.07 to 27.55)."
  },
  {
   "id": "E2.c7",
   "beat": "E2",
   "c": 7,
   "s": 257.6,
   "e": 261.2,
   "text": "The three failing nights recover to 82.54%, 88.68% and 84.87%."
  },
  {
   "id": "E3.c1",
   "beat": "E3",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 262.1,
   "e": 268.5,
   "text": "But R1 reads each test night's unlabeled signal and was chosen after the baseline failed: offline, post-hoc."
  },
  {
   "id": "E3.c2",
   "beat": "E3",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 269.2,
   "e": 276.6,
   "text": "Whole-recording statistics give 71.84%: −3.02 versus R1, interval −7.12 to +1.22. No clear difference, not proof of equality."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2.c1",
   "beat": "L2",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 277.4,
   "e": 283.2,
   "text": "What we cannot say: that it works on new people, since R1 is offline and was chosen after the fact."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2.c2",
   "beat": "L2",
   "c": 2,
   "s": 283.9,
   "e": 289.8,
   "text": "Nor that 80.99% beats or matches the paper's 83%, that it is clinically ready, or that N1 is solved."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2.c3",
   "beat": "L2",
   "c": 3,
   "s": 290.5,
   "e": 294.1,
   "text": "None of the 78 audited participants is certified independent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3.c1",
   "beat": "L3",
   "c": 1,
   "s": 294.9,
   "e": 302,
   "text": "Nights differ, and an average can hide the one that fails. Next: lock the protocol, then test on truly independent data."
  }
 ]
}
